# What digging a node yields — design.md §2, server half.
#
# §2 splits a node definition in two and puts drops on the **server** side,
# because they are what mod code varies: "protection vetoes a dig, `on_dig`
# replaces the node with something else, stone drops cobble" (§7.1). Nothing a
# client is told about a node includes what it drops, and §7.1 step 3 is
# explicit that a client must not predict one.
#
# It lives in `core/` anyway, and that is a statement about M5 rather than about
# the split. In-process singleplayer *is* the server (§10, §7.1's "the server
# call is synchronous over the in-memory channel"), so the process holding the
# client holds both halves. **M6 is where this file stops being reachable from
# the client**, and it is written as its own module rather than as a column on
# §2's registry precisely so that moving it is a matter of who imports it.
#
# ## A node drops itself unless told otherwise
#
# The table holds exceptions, not entries. A default of "nothing" would mean a
# node whose drop nobody remembered to register vanishes when dug, silently,
# which is the failure mode a placeholder content set is most likely to have —
# and the one hardest to notice, because the node does disappear, which is half
# of what a dig looks like.
#
# So `drop_item` answers *the node's own item* for anything unregistered, and
# the table is only for the interesting cases: grass drops dirt, and later stone
# drops cobble and an ore drops a lump.
#
# **The default moved when §2's items arrived.** It used to be `id` — a node
# dropping its own content id, back when an item id was a content id. It is now
# `item_of_node`, which is the same sentence in the new vocabulary and is why
# this function takes the item registry: "a node drops itself" has to be
# resolved somewhere, and one place is better than every call site.

(mod drops ^profile web)

(import [ItemRegistry item_of_node no_item] from "./item.gene")

(let max_content 4096.0)

# Three parallel columns and a flag, indexed by content id, which is
# `core/registry.gene`'s layout for the same reason: a lookup is an indexed read
# rather than a map probe. `F32` throughout, per `core/world.gene`'s rule.
(type DropTable
  ^props {^declared (Buffer F32)
          ^item     (Buffer F32)
          ^count    (Buffer F32)}

  (ctor []
    (set self/declared ($buffer F32 max_content))
    (set self/item ($buffer F32 max_content))
    (set self/count ($buffer F32 max_content))))

(fn new_drop_table [] : DropTable
  (new DropTable))

# Declare that digging `id` yields `count` of `item`. A `count` of 0 is a node
# that drops nothing, which is a real thing to want — upstream's leaves do it —
# and is why "declared" is its own column rather than being inferred from a
# count.
(fn register_drop [t : DropTable id : F64 item : F64 count : F64] : Nil
  (t/declared .set id 1.0)
  (t/item .set id item)
  (t/count .set id count))

(fn declared? [t : DropTable id : F64] : Bool
  (== (t/declared .get id) 1.0))

(fn drop_item [t : DropTable items : ItemRegistry id : F64] : F64
  (if (declared? t id) (t/item .get id) (item_of_node items id)))

# A node with no item of its own — §1's reserved ids, or one a mod opted out of
# with `^is_item false` — yields nothing, whatever the count says. Without this
# a dig on such a node would add `no_item` to the hotbar and the hotbar would
# hold a stack of -1.
(fn drop_count [t : DropTable items : ItemRegistry id : F64] : F64
  (if (== (drop_item t items id) no_item) 0.0
    (if (declared? t id) (t/count .get id) 1.0)))
